WEBVTT

NOTE SE201 Lesson 2: Build the whole system, AI-native — captions generated from the narration script

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.372
Software engineering 201. From loop, to enterprise systems.

2
00:00:05.372 --> 00:00:09.170
Lesson two. Build the whole system, AI native.

3
00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:14.441
Quick question to start. You're asking agents to build a whole app, not just one fix.

4
00:00:14.441 --> 00:00:16.641
What matters most? The smartest model?

5
00:00:16.641 --> 00:00:21.562
The longest prompt? Or a system the agents can read, check, and rebuild on their own?

6
00:00:21.562 --> 00:00:27.178
The system they can read. A smarter model still guesses when the project lives in someone's head.

7
00:00:27.178 --> 00:00:29.320
A long prompt is gone after one chat.

8
00:00:29.320 --> 00:00:33.430
What's written into the system is there for every run, and every agent.

9
00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:36.830
In 101, the unit of work was one loop.

10
00:00:36.830 --> 00:00:39.158
Plan, build, verify. One fix.

11
00:00:39.158 --> 00:00:43.333
One feature. In 201, the unit of work is the system.

12
00:00:43.333 --> 00:00:46.704
Many parts. Many people. Years of history.

13
00:00:46.704 --> 00:00:51.280
And now, agents working in it right alongside the people.

14
00:00:51.630 --> 00:00:54.107
So what does AI native actually mean?

15
00:00:54.107 --> 00:00:56.719
Here's the definition this series uses.

16
00:00:56.719 --> 00:01:04.887
A system is AI native when agents and people can both read it, find their way around it, contribute to it, and rebuild it.

17
00:01:04.887 --> 00:01:07.097
Not only the person who wrote it.

18
00:01:07.097 --> 00:01:09.440
Not only whoever knows the history.

19
00:01:09.800 --> 00:01:11.541
That takes four things. One.

20
00:01:11.541 --> 00:01:20.616
Every page people read has a twin an agent can read: plain Markdown, an index of the whole system, and an agents file that says how to work there.

21
00:01:20.616 --> 00:01:22.916
Two. Specs and briefs are the source.

22
00:01:22.916 --> 00:01:25.403
Change the brief, and the build follows.

23
00:01:25.403 --> 00:01:27.951
Three. Tests and checks are the contract.

24
00:01:27.951 --> 00:01:32.054
An agent is done when the checks pass, not when it says it's done.

25
00:01:32.054 --> 00:01:38.270
And four. Every change goes to staging first, and a person approves it before it reaches production.

26
00:01:38.620 --> 00:01:40.104
And this isn't hypothetical.

27
00:01:40.104 --> 00:01:43.020
The site you're on right now is built exactly this way.

28
00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:48.509
Every page has an index.md twin, and every folder has an AGENTS.md file.

29
00:01:48.509 --> 00:01:58.021
There's an llms.txt that indexes the whole site, a machine-readable lesson catalog, and a read-only connector AI tools can plug into.

30
00:01:58.021 --> 00:02:06.960
The pages are generated from one content file, and the rebuild steps are written down, so an agent can regenerate every page.

31
00:02:07.310 --> 00:02:09.631
And here's the part that matters most.

32
00:02:09.631 --> 00:02:12.562
Agents open pull requests into a staging branch.

33
00:02:12.562 --> 00:02:16.105
Staging is a full preview of the site, hidden from search.

34
00:02:16.105 --> 00:02:18.120
And only I merge into production.

35
00:02:18.470 --> 00:02:20.813
Think of a kitchen with one great cook.

36
00:02:20.813 --> 00:02:22.615
The recipes are in their head.

37
00:02:22.615 --> 00:02:24.718
Only they know where everything is.

38
00:02:24.718 --> 00:02:26.520
And every plate waits on them.

39
00:02:26.860 --> 00:02:28.948
Now picture a big restaurant kitchen.

40
00:02:28.948 --> 00:02:30.585
The recipes are written down.

41
00:02:30.585 --> 00:02:34.988
Every station is labeled. A cook on a brand new shift can work from the cards.

42
00:02:34.988 --> 00:02:38.375
And nothing leaves the pass until the head chef looks at it.

43
00:02:38.375 --> 00:02:41.310
That's what lets a kitchen grow past one great cook.

44
00:02:41.670 --> 00:02:44.945
Now scale that up. An enterprise system isn't one repo.

45
00:02:44.945 --> 00:02:51.494
It's dozens of services, data that has to stay private, and people who need to know who changed what, and why.

46
00:02:51.494 --> 00:02:53.399
The same four things still hold.

47
00:02:53.399 --> 00:02:59.770
They just matter more. Without them, every agent starts from zero, and every person becomes the bottleneck.

48
00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:02.255
That's the path for this series.

49
00:03:02.255 --> 00:03:04.990
Today: build the whole system, AI native.

50
00:03:04.990 --> 00:03:07.126
Then, decompose it into a graph.

51
00:03:07.126 --> 00:03:10.928
Run agents in parallel, and bring the work back together.

52
00:03:10.928 --> 00:03:13.464
Put people where the decisions matter.

53
00:03:13.464 --> 00:03:15.732
Measure quality, and watch it run.

54
00:03:15.732 --> 00:03:17.801
Turn repeated work into skills.

55
00:03:17.801 --> 00:03:20.202
Wire it into routines and pipelines.

56
00:03:20.202 --> 00:03:26.341
Then, agents that operate enterprise systems, with real permissions, and a full audit trail.

57
00:03:26.341 --> 00:03:29.810
And last, a capstone: one feature, built end to end.

58
00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:32.600
Which gives us rule two, for your agents.

59
00:03:32.600 --> 00:03:39.208
Before you build anything bigger than one fix, make it readable: a Markdown twin, an index, and an agents file.

60
00:03:39.208 --> 00:03:42.839
Keep the specs as the source, and the checks as the contract.

61
00:03:42.839 --> 00:03:47.660
And send every change through staging, for a person to approve before production.

62
00:03:48.020 --> 00:03:50.543
Now, your turn. Pick a system you work on.

63
00:03:50.543 --> 00:03:54.148
If a brand new agent showed up tomorrow, what could it read?

64
00:03:54.148 --> 00:03:56.070
And what would it have to guess?

65
00:03:56.410 --> 00:03:58.580
See you in lesson three.
